Original commit body from d2ce0180ae21904c27cd7eca2dd1d75526e7c8b7:

WI-vihop: a `fs.promises.<fn>(...)` call now reaches the `fs/promises` catalogue
rows instead of stopping at a bare `fs` module or a short-name guess.

THE GAP. The analyzer resolved `fs.<fn>` and `require('fs/promises').<fn>`, but the
member chain `fs.promises.<fn>` — the idiomatic spelling — fell through: the receiver
is a `member_expression`, not an identifier, so no namespace lookup fired and the
edge never named the promises submodule.

THE FIX. `_promises_submodule` recognises `<ns>.promises` where `<ns>` is a name bound
by an ESM namespace import, an ESM default import, or a CJS `require(...)`, and emits
`<module>/promises` when the base module is one that actually has a promises submodule
(`fs`, `dns`, `stream`, `readline`, `timers`). The allowlist is the point: without it
the rule would mint `<anything>/promises` for any object with a `promises` property.

PROBE FIRST, THEN FIX. The item listed four spellings. A fifth — the ESM default
import `import fs from 'fs'` — was equally broken, and it is the one Next.js actually
uses. All five are covered.

MEASURED, three repos, one survey per arm (an analyzer change moves the survey):
  +303 edges reach a catalogue row — podman-desktop 53->127, vscode 12->151,
  nextjs 276->366 — with `calls_total` IDENTICAL in all three, so this RE-LABELS
  edges and mints none.

ATTRIBUTION OF THE ONE ANOMALY. 24 of nextjs's 90 were previously bound to
`CacheFs.mkdir` — an INTERFACE DECLARATION in an unrelated file, matched by Case 4's
low-confidence short-name path. Those are a PRECISION gain, not a theft from a
correct binding.

Tests: 11 new in test_js_promises_submodule_chain.py, including the allowlist
rejection. Full suite green, 100% coverage on the changed file, mypy strict ratchet
